#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Fail if a user-visible string calls this product Zap, Oz, or Warp.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# ---------------
# The fork shipped three names for one thing. On `repin` before this guard:
# "Zap Agent" x25, "Warp Agent" x23, "Phosphor Agent" x7, plus 141 occurrences
# of Oz. Upstream's own "Oz Agent" -> "Warp Agent" rename (42effe840) is what
# surfaced it, and every port from the pin drags more of Warp's copy in.
#
# This is not housekeeping. docs/DESIGN-PHOSPHOR-FORK.md §6: AGPL-3.0 §7
# declines trademark grants, so shipping Warp's name on this fork's UI is a
# licensing problem, not just an inconsistent one.
#
# WHAT IT CHECKS, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT
# -------------------------------------------------
# Only text that reaches a human: Rust string literals in shipped source, and
# the VALUE side of the Fluent catalogues. `warp` is also 24 crate names, 22
# workspace dependencies, hundreds of Rust identifiers, the `WARP_*` env vars,
# the OSC/DCS payloads, the `warpify` shell hooks, and every legitimate
# reference to upstream in docs/. A blanket search over any of those produces
# noise, and a guard people learn to ignore is worse than no guard. So the
# match is deliberately narrow:
#
#   1. Only the three capitalised brand words, and only where the characters
#      around them say "prose" rather than "symbol". A hit must not be preceded
#      by [A-Za-z0-9_.:-] nor followed by [A-Z0-9_-]. That predicate keeps the
#      guard quiet on:
#        WARP_SKILL_DIRS, WARP.md      (all-caps -- different case, no match)
#        warp_tui, warpdotdev, warp.dev (lowercase -- no match)
#        WarpTheme, WarpAgent, ZapDrive (followed by an UPPERCASE letter)
#        openWarp, AskWarpAI, SshWarpifyBlock (preceded by a letter)
#        Harness::Oz, Icon::Zap          (preceded by ':')
#        dev.warp.Warp, dev.zap.Zap      (preceded by '.')
#        X-Zap-Client-ID, Zap-Run-GeneratorCommand (adjacent '-')
#
#      The asymmetry between the two sides is the point, and it was learned the
#      hard way. The first version of this guard excluded a following LETTER of
#      either case, and that is precisely the hole five shipped strings walked
#      through: "Get Zapping" x2 and "Get Warping" (onboarding's primary
#      button), "the rest of your Warpified experience" (the SSH banner), and
#      ja's `session-config-get-warping` = "Warping を開始", which no purge pass
#      had ever touched. A brand word inflected by an English suffix is still
#      branding -- arguably the worst kind, because it reads as native product
#      voice.
#
#      What separates those from `WarpTheme` is CASE AT THE BOUNDARY, not a
#      list of words. Rust identifiers that embed a brand word are CamelCase,
#      so the next character is uppercase; an inflected English word continues
#      in lowercase. Every brand-adjacent literal in the tree obeys this:
#        lowercase continuation, all shipped copy -- Zapping, Warping, Warpified
#        uppercase continuation, all symbols     -- WarpWindow, WarpHostView,
#          WarpDrivePrivacySettings, ZapDockTilePlugin, ZapLaunchModal
#      The leading side needs no such relaxation: nothing in English ends in
#      "...Warp", so a preceding letter always means an identifier
#      (openWarp, SourcedRcFileForWarp) and that exclusion stays absolute.
#
#   2. In Rust, only inside a string literal, never a comment. An identifier
#      or a doc comment mentioning Warp is lineage, not branding. The literals
#      are found with a scanner rather than a line regex, because Rust strings
#      span lines: an earlier line-based draft of this guard silently missed
#      eight shipped strings, including five "reinstall Warp Agent CLI" errors
#      broken across a backslash continuation.
#
#   3. In Rust, a literal with NO whitespace in it is a token, not copy. Keys,
#      headers, registry paths, single-instance mutex names, DCS markers and
#      bare app-identity names ("Zap" in `paths.rs`'s legacy directory map)
#      are all single tokens; user-visible copy is at minimum a two-word label.
#
#   4. In Rust, a literal in a match-arm pattern or on the right of == / != is
#      an input being RECOGNISED, not output being SHOWN. That is what keeps
#      `SettingsSection::from_str`'s "Oz" | "Zap Agent" legacy aliases legal --
#      renaming those orphans every settings row already persisted under them.
#
#   5. Tests are excluded: *_test.rs, *_tests.rs, tests/, examples/,
#      test_data/, the `crates/integration` harness crate, and
#      `app/src/integration_testing`. Their brand strings are arbitrary fixture
#      data, not shipped copy. `integration_testing` lives under app/src rather
#      than in tests/, but it is the same thing as `crates/integration` -- its
#      brand hits are `TestStep::new("Assert the Warpify banner is visible")`
#      and an `AssertionOutcome::failure` message, which name the mechanism in
#      text only a test author reads.
#
# THE ONE PLACE SHAPE IS NOT ENOUGH
# ---------------------------------
# Two uses of a standalone "Warp" in the catalogues are legitimate and no
# string-shape rule can tell them from a regression, because they are correct
# for reasons that live outside the text:
#
#   - "Warp plugin" / "Warp extension" / "Warp marketplace" (and the ja/zh
#     forms) name real third-party packages -- @warp-dot-dev/opencode-warp and
#     warpdotdev/codex-warp. Renaming them stops the user finding what the
#     instructions tell them to install. Handled by a phrase rule below:
#     "Warp" is allowed when it immediately qualifies a package noun.
#
#   - The AGPL-3.0 §13 attribution ("Phosphor is a modified version of Warp")
#     must stay true. Handled by an in-band marker, NOT a list in this file.
#
# WHERE THE MARKER GOES -- it must land in a COMMENT, never in the text
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
#   Fluent: `# brand-guard: allow <reason>` on the comment line DIRECTLY ABOVE
#           the message. Fluent has no trailing-comment syntax, so "on the same
#           line" is not a thing: anything after the `=` is the value the user
#           reads.
#   Rust:   `// brand-guard: allow <reason>` on the line ABOVE the statement,
#           or trailing after the literal's CLOSING quote.
#
# An earlier version searched for the marker in the same text it was excusing.
# That made the marker self-fulfilling: any string whose own content contained
# the phrase "brand-guard: allow" silenced its own hit, in both Rust and
# Fluent. It also documented a Rust form ("on or directly above the line") of
# which only the Fluent half was ever implemented. The marker is now read only
# from comment context -- the line above the literal, or a `//` comment after
# it -- so a string can no longer excuse itself.
#
# The marker is in-band on purpose. A suppression list inside a guard drifts
# and is invisible at the point of change; a marker is reviewed in the same
# diff as the string it excuses. If this file ever grows a THIRD content rule,
# that is the signal the shape-based boundary has stopped working -- reopen it
# rather than adding a fourth.
#
# WHAT THIS GUARD CANNOT SEE
# --------------------------
# Copy assembled at runtime from a non-literal source, and strings that reach
# the user through a channel this scope does not cover (packaging metadata,
# .desktop entries, generated changelogs). Those still need a human.

set -uo pipefail
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."

if ! command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "brand strings: skipped (python3 not available)"
    exit 0
fi

python3 - "$@" <<'PY'
import re
import sys
from pathlib import Path

# Trailing class excludes UPPERCASE only: a following lowercase letter is an
# English inflection ("Zapping", "Warpified") and must be caught, while a
# following uppercase letter is CamelCase ("WarpTheme") and must not be. See
# rule 1 in the header for why the two sides are deliberately asymmetric.
TOKEN = re.compile(r"(?<![A-Za-z0-9_.:\-])(Zap|Oz|Warp)(?![A-Z0-9_\-])")
STRING = re.compile(r'"((?:[^"\\]|\\.)*)"')
MARKER = re.compile(r"brand-guard:\s*allow")

# Rule 4: the literal is being matched against, not rendered.
MATCH_ARM = re.compile(r'^\s*(?:r#)?"(?:[^"\\]|\\.)*"(?:\s*\|\s*(?:r#)?"(?:[^"\\]|\\.)*")*\s*=>')
COMPARISON = re.compile(r"[=!]=\s*(?:&\s*)?(?:r#)?$")

# Rule "the one place shape is not enough": Warp qualifying a package noun.
PACKAGE_NOUN = re.compile(
    r"Warp\s*(?:plugin|extension|marketplace|"
    r"プラグイン|"          # プラグイン
    r"拡張機能|"                # 拡張機能 (ja: extension)
    r"插件|"                            # 插件 (zh: plugin)
    r"扩展)",                           # 扩展 (zh: extension)
    re.IGNORECASE,
)

SKIP_DIR_PARTS = {"target", "node_modules", "test_data", "tests", "examples"}


def rust_sources():
    for root in ("app/src", "crates"):
        for p in sorted(Path(root).rglob("*.rs")):
            parts = set(p.parts)
            if parts & SKIP_DIR_PARTS:
                continue
            if p.parts[:2] == ("crates", "integration"):
                continue
            if p.parts[:3] == ("app", "src", "integration_testing"):
                continue
            if p.name.endswith(("_test.rs", "_tests.rs")):
                continue
            yield p


def rust_literals(text):
    """Yield (start_offset, end_offset, contents) for every Rust string literal.

    This is a scanner, not a line regex, because Rust string literals span
    lines -- both `"...\\` backslash continuations and multi-line `r#"..."#`
    raw strings. An earlier line-based version of this guard missed ten
    user-visible "Warp Agent CLI" errors in `warp_tui/src/autoupdate.rs` for
    exactly that reason, which is the whole argument for scanning properly.
    Comments and char literals are skipped so a `"` inside either cannot
    desynchronise the scan for the rest of the file.
    """
    i, n = 0, len(text)
    while i < n:
        c = text[i]
        if c == "/" and i + 1 < n and text[i + 1] == "/":
            j = text.find("\n", i)
            i = n if j < 0 else j + 1
        elif c == "/" and i + 1 < n and text[i + 1] == "*":
            depth, i = 1, i + 2
            while i < n and depth:
                if text.startswith("/*", i):
                    depth, i = depth + 1, i + 2
                elif text.startswith("*/", i):
                    depth, i = depth - 1, i + 2
                else:
                    i += 1
        elif c == "'" and re.match(r"'(?:\\.|[^\\'])'", text[i : i + 4]):
            i += len(re.match(r"'(?:\\.|[^\\'])'", text[i : i + 4]).group(0))
        elif c == "r" and (m := re.match(r'r(#*)"', text[i:])):
            hashes = m.group(1)
            start = i + len(m.group(0))
            close = text.find('"' + hashes, start)
            if close < 0:
                return
            yield i, close + 1 + len(hashes), text[start:close]
            i = close + 1 + len(hashes)
        elif c == '"':
            j = i + 1
            while j < n:
                if text[j] == "\\":
                    j += 2
                elif text[j] == '"':
                    break
                else:
                    j += 1
            if j >= n:
                return
            yield i, j + 1, text[i + 1 : j]
            i = j + 1
        else:
            i += 1


def check_rust():
    hits = []
    for p in rust_sources():
        try:
            text = p.read_text(errors="replace")
        except OSError:
            continue
        line_starts = [0] + [m.end() for m in re.finditer(r"\n", text)]
        lines = text.splitlines()

        def lineno_of(off):
            lo, hi = 0, len(line_starts) - 1
            while lo < hi:
                mid = (lo + hi + 1) // 2
                if line_starts[mid] <= off:
                    lo = mid
                else:
                    hi = mid - 1
            return lo + 1

        for start, end, literal in rust_literals(text):
            if not TOKEN.search(literal):
                continue
            if not re.search(r"\s", literal):
                continue  # rule 3: a token, not a sentence
            lineno = lineno_of(start)
            # The marker is read ONLY from comment context, never from the
            # literal itself -- otherwise a string containing the phrase
            # "brand-guard: allow" excuses itself. Two accepted positions: a
            # `//` comment on the line above the literal opens, or a trailing
            # `//` comment after the literal's closing quote.
            above = lines[lineno - 2] if lineno >= 2 else ""
            eol = text.find("\n", end)
            after = text[end : len(text) if eol < 0 else eol]
            if above.lstrip().startswith("//") and MARKER.search(above):
                continue
            if "//" in after and MARKER.search(after[after.index("//") :]):
                continue
            line = lines[lineno - 1] if lineno <= len(lines) else ""
            if MATCH_ARM.match(line):
                continue  # rule 4
            prefix = line[: start - line_starts[lineno - 1]]
            if COMPARISON.search(prefix):
                continue  # rule 4
            flat = " ".join(literal.split())
            hits.append((str(p), lineno, f'"{flat[:120]}"'))
    return hits


FTL_ID = re.compile(r"^(\s*\.?[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*\s*=\s*)(.*)$")


def check_fluent():
    hits = []
    for p in sorted(Path("app/i18n").glob("*/*.ftl")):
        lines = p.read_text(errors="replace").splitlines()
        for lineno, line in enumerate(lines, 1):
            if not line.strip() or line.lstrip().startswith("#"):
                continue
            m = FTL_ID.match(line)
            if m:
                value = m.group(2)
            elif line[:1].isspace():
                value = line  # continuation of a multi-line value
            else:
                continue
            if not TOKEN.search(value):
                continue
            # Comment line directly above ONLY. `MARKER.search(line)` used to
            # be accepted too, but in Fluent everything after the `=` is the
            # user-visible value, so that let a message excuse itself simply by
            # containing the phrase.
            prev = lines[lineno - 2] if lineno >= 2 else ""
            if prev.lstrip().startswith("#") and MARKER.search(prev):
                continue
            # Strip the legitimate package-noun uses, then re-test.
            if not TOKEN.search(PACKAGE_NOUN.sub("", value)):
                continue
            hits.append((str(p), lineno, value.strip()))
    return hits


def check_prose():
    """Files the user opens by hand, where the comment IS the user-visible part."""
    hits = []
    targets = sorted(Path("themes").glob("*.yaml"))
    targets += sorted(Path("app/resources/tab_configs").glob("*.toml"))
    for p in targets:
        lines = p.read_text(errors="replace").splitlines()
        for lineno, line in enumerate(lines, 1):
            if not TOKEN.search(line):
                continue
            # In these files the comment IS the user-visible part, so a marker
            # on the same line would be excusing itself. It goes on the line
            # above, as in the Fluent catalogues.
            prev = lines[lineno - 2] if lineno >= 2 else ""
            if prev.lstrip().startswith("#") and MARKER.search(prev):
                continue
            hits.append((str(p), lineno, line.strip()))
    return hits


status = 0
for label, hits in (
    ("Rust string literal", check_rust()),
    ("Fluent message value", check_fluent()),
    ("shipped prose file", check_prose()),
):
    if not hits:
        continue
    status = 1
    print(f"error: {len(hits)} user-visible {label}(s) name the product Zap/Oz/Warp:", file=sys.stderr)
    for path, lineno, text in hits:
        print(f"  {path}:{lineno}  {text[:140]}", file=sys.stderr)
    print(file=sys.stderr)

if status:
    print(
        "The product is Phosphor and its agent is Phosphor Agent. If a hit above is\n"
        "genuinely not a brand reference -- an upstream attribution, or the name of a\n"
        "Warp-published package the user has to find -- mark it with\n"
        "`brand-guard: allow <reason>` rather than editing this script. The marker must\n"
        "be in a COMMENT, because a marker inside the text would excuse itself:\n"
        "  Fluent / themes / tab_configs:  `# brand-guard: allow ...` on the line ABOVE\n"
        "  Rust:  `// brand-guard: allow ...` on the line above, or trailing after the\n"
        "         literal's closing quote.",
        file=sys.stderr,
    )
    sys.exit(1)

print("check_brand_strings: ok -- no user-visible string names Zap, Oz, or Warp")
PY
